  "dom_id": "step-q352955",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222139/assets/0e402d36061211a9e51df852f7fa595eb212e5c6ce9b3d43a2d53f9bea1fedd3.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=(x-1)^{4}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222139/assets/2a2e4be26d18e408bf6e4580c72ec2f3c08eed1750d38e0716f06a32d3c03e7c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,1-(x-1)^{4}\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-2(x-1)^{4}+(x-1)^{8})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(x-\\frac{2}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{9}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([2-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(1)^{9}\\text{ }]-[0-\\frac{2}{5}(-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(-1)^{9}\\text{ }]) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
